{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-122474" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the domain of the vector field $F$ on ${R}^{2},$ given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\,j\\,.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061075/state.json" "file_sha256" "8dbdc11e07d0d77b7b467c70fd982c465e564995c846314f14b206b479b0c1e5"} {"question" "q-122474" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function $F$ can be expressed in terms of its components $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\\,Q(x,y)=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe first find the domain of each component function. Then, we compute their intersection to find the domain of $F:$\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined everywhere other than when the denominator vanishes. To find where the denominator vanishes, we set it equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,x=y=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $P$ is given by\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,(x,y)\\ne (0,0)}.\n$$\n\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined everywhere other than when the denominator vanishes. To find where the denominator vanishes, we set it equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,y=\\pm x\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $Q$ is given by\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,y\\ne \\pm x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $F$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={D}_{1}\\cap {D}_{2}={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,y\\ne \\pm x}\\,.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061075/state.json" "file_sha256" "8dbdc11e07d0d77b7b467c70fd982c465e564995c846314f14b206b479b0c1e5"} {"question" "q-122474" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061075/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9d80d025c32e0d44ebf099744af67e92e8d9f9aedee3e55e1ff6a27af4a8b3c0"} {"question" "q-122474" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061075/state.json" "file_sha256" "8dbdc11e07d0d77b7b467c70fd982c465e564995c846314f14b206b479b0c1e5"} {"question" "q-122474" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,(x,y)\\ne 0}"] ["math" "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,y\\ne \\pm x}"] ["math" "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,y\\ne x}"] ["math" "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<1}"] ["math" "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ge 1}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061075/state.json" "file_sha256" "8dbdc11e07d0d77b7b467c70fd982c465e564995c846314f14b206b479b0c1e5"} {"question" "q-122474" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,y\\ne \\pm x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061075/state.json" "file_sha256" "8dbdc11e07d0d77b7b467c70fd982c465e564995c846314f14b206b479b0c1e5"} {"question" "q-122660" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the domain of the vector field $F$ on ${R}^{2},$ given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4})\\,i+\\frac{xy}{x-1}\\,j\\,.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061075/state.json" "file_sha256" "8dbdc11e07d0d77b7b467c70fd982c465e564995c846314f14b206b479b0c1e5"} {"question" "q-122660" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function $F$ can be expressed in terms of its components $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4}),\\,Q(x,y)=\\frac{xy}{x-1}.\n$$\n\nWe first find the domain of each component function. Then, we compute their intersection to find the domain of $F:$\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined everywhere other than when the denominator vanishes. To find where the denominator vanishes, we set it equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4=0\\,⟹\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $P$ is given by\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 4}.\n$$\n\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined everywhere other than when the denominator vanishes. To find where the denominator vanishes, we set it equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\nx-1=0\\,⟹\\,x=1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $Q$ is given by\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x\\ne 1}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $F$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={D}_{1}\\cap {D}_{2}={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 4,\\,x\\ne 1}\\,.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061075/state.json" "file_sha256" "8dbdc11e07d0d77b7b467c70fd982c465e564995c846314f14b206b479b0c1e5"} {"question" "q-122660" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061075/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9d80d025c32e0d44ebf099744af67e92e8d9f9aedee3e55e1ff6a27af4a8b3c0"} {"question" "q-122660" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061075/state.json" "file_sha256" "8dbdc11e07d0d77b7b467c70fd982c465e564995c846314f14b206b479b0c1e5"} {"question" "q-122660" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<4,\\,x\\ne 1}"] ["math" "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<4}"] ["math" "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>4}"] ["math" "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 4,\\,x\\ne 1}"] ["math" "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061075/state.json" "file_sha256" "8dbdc11e07d0d77b7b467c70fd982c465e564995c846314f14b206b479b0c1e5"} {"question" "q-122660" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 4,\\,x\\ne 1}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061075/state.json" "file_sha256" "8dbdc11e07d0d77b7b467c70fd982c465e564995c846314f14b206b479b0c1e5"} {"question" "q-122685" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{3}?$\n\n- $T(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,j+{z}^{2}\\,k$\n- $U(t)=2t\\,i-{t}^{2}\\,j-\\sqrt{t}\\,k$\n- $V(x,y,z)={e}^{-2z}\\sqrt{x+y}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061075/state.json" "file_sha256" "8dbdc11e07d0d77b7b467c70fd982c465e564995c846314f14b206b479b0c1e5"} {"question" "q-122685" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{3}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{3}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $F(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $T(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns every point $(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}$ to a vector in ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $U(t)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in $R$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $V(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{3}$ to functions in $R$ (not ${R}^{3}$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061075/state.json" "file_sha256" "8dbdc11e07d0d77b7b467c70fd982c465e564995c846314f14b206b479b0c1e5"} {"question" "q-122685" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061075/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9d80d025c32e0d44ebf099744af67e92e8d9f9aedee3e55e1ff6a27af4a8b3c0"} {"question" "q-122685" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061075/state.json" "file_sha256" "8dbdc11e07d0d77b7b467c70fd982c465e564995c846314f14b206b479b0c1e5"} {"question" "q-122685" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061075/state.json" "file_sha256" "8dbdc11e07d0d77b7b467c70fd982c465e564995c846314f14b206b479b0c1e5"} {"question" "q-122685" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061075/state.json" "file_sha256" "8dbdc11e07d0d77b7b467c70fd982c465e564995c846314f14b206b479b0c1e5"} {"question" "q-122691" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{3}?$\n\n- $U(x,y,z)=(x+2){e}^{yz}$\n- $V(x,y,z)=⟨5{e}^{-{z}^{2}},{x}^{3}y,1-{y}^{3}⟩$\n- $W(x,y)=⟨x-3{y}^{2},2-y⟩$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061075/state.json" "file_sha256" "8dbdc11e07d0d77b7b467c70fd982c465e564995c846314f14b206b479b0c1e5"} {"question" "q-122691" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{3}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{3}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $F(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $U(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{3}$ to functions in $R$ (not ${R}^{3}$).\n\n- The function $V(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns every point $(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}$ to a vector in ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $W(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061075/state.json" "file_sha256" "8dbdc11e07d0d77b7b467c70fd982c465e564995c846314f14b206b479b0c1e5"} {"question" "q-122691" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061075/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9d80d025c32e0d44ebf099744af67e92e8d9f9aedee3e55e1ff6a27af4a8b3c0"} {"question" "q-122691" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061075/state.json" "file_sha256" "8dbdc11e07d0d77b7b467c70fd982c465e564995c846314f14b206b479b0c1e5"} {"question" "q-122691" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061075/state.json" "file_sha256" "8dbdc11e07d0d77b7b467c70fd982c465e564995c846314f14b206b479b0c1e5"} {"question" "q-122691" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061075/state.json" "file_sha256" "8dbdc11e07d0d77b7b467c70fd982c465e564995c846314f14b206b479b0c1e5"} {"question" "q-122710" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine which of the following shaded regions depicts the domain of the vector field $F$ on ${R}^{2},$ given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-{x}^{2})\\,i+x\\sqrt{1-|y|}\\,j\\,.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061075/state.json" "file_sha256" "8dbdc11e07d0d77b7b467c70fd982c465e564995c846314f14b206b479b0c1e5"} {"question" "q-122710" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function $F$ can be expressed in terms of its components $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-{x}^{2}),\\,Q(x,y)=x\\sqrt{1-|y|}.\n$$\n\nWe first find the domain of each component function. Then, we compute their intersection to find the domain of $F:$\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined when the expression under the logarithm is positive. So, to find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n1-{x}^{2}>0\\,⟹\\,-1<x<1.\n$$\n\nThis defines the region ${D}_{1},$ shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061075/assets/89c78ec587826c15c90ef4ffdfc0fd9f8af3d7b4d3d1cc099ce24b883b9842c2.png)\n\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined the expression under the radical is positive or equal to zero. So, to find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n1-|y|\\ge 0\\,⟹\\,-1\\le y\\le 1.\n$$\n\nThis defines the region ${D}_{2},$ shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061075/assets/addd649aa739711fd34ebd3e82579c8c0100a2b9a109d5cafabde750bc94eb9a.png)\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $F$ is given by ${D}_{1}\\cap {D}_{2},$ shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061075/assets/49000dd6905017bc64c9a67b6d8a2ea475a684477ac6e02d6ed15c4c7dbbb3aa.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061075/state.json" "file_sha256" "8dbdc11e07d0d77b7b467c70fd982c465e564995c846314f14b206b479b0c1e5"} {"question" "q-122710" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061075/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9d80d025c32e0d44ebf099744af67e92e8d9f9aedee3e55e1ff6a27af4a8b3c0"} {"question" "q-122710" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivar